# AN ARCH LINUX MICROVM RUNNING SWAY ON A VIRTIO-GPU DISPLAY.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS GUEST IS THE SCREEN. The minimal arch image proves a kernel finds a disk;
# this one proves a Wayland compositor draws on a machine whose only GPU is virtio's, and
# that the pixels can be read back.
#
# HOW IT DRAWS:
#
#	virtio-gpu    qemu's paravirtual display, attached as `virtio-gpu-pci` because the MMIO
#	              form never binds (MEASURED: status 0x83, NEEDS_RESET, no /dev/dri/card0).
#	              On the PCIe bus the guest gets card0 (with a Virtual-1 output) and
#	              renderD128 together - a KMS output and a render node on one device.
#	sway          wlroots' `drm` backend driving that device with the `pixman` renderer -
#	              pure CPU rasterisation, no EGL on hardware.
#	easystream    reads the compositor's frames through `wlr-screencopy`, a protocol sway
#	              implements itself, and serves them as `est://` on 48079.
#
# NO XWAYLAND AND NO X SERVER. The session is pure Wayland; there is no X client in the
# image, which is why nothing here mentions DISPLAY.
#
# THE ASSERTION IS TEXT THE COMPOSITOR PAINTS ITSELF. sway draws a bar whose status command
# echoes a fixed string, so seeing it means sway started, found an output, and rendered.

walkthrough "arch sway microvm - a wayland compositor on virtio-gpu"
resolution 1024x768
recording_resolution 1024x768
step "needs arch-vmlinuz arch-initramfs arch.qcow2"
  needs [arch-vmlinuz, arch-initramfs, arch.qcow2]

step "disk arch.qcow2"
  disk arch.qcow2

# `with a desktop` ASKS FOR THE GPU: the machine type turns PCIe on and attaches
# `virtio-gpu-pci`, which is the transport the driver actually binds to. `console=ttyS0`
# is where the boot log and the login prompt are read.
step "boot as a microvm from kernel \"arch-vmlinuz\" and initramfs \"arch-initramfs\" with \"console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4\" with a desktop with memory 1536, waiting up to 240s"
  boot
  initrd arch-initramfs
  kernel arch-vmlinuz
  kernel_append "console=ttyS0 root=/dev/vda rw rootfstype=ext4"
  memory_mb 1536
  microvm
  microvm_display desktop
  os microvm
  timeout 240

# THE FIRST FACT: the kernel found the disk and userspace started.
step "the console will say \"login\""
  until login
  from console
  timeout 180

# AND THE SESSION IS vmuser's, not root's. PipeWire refuses root outright
# (`ConditionUser=!root`), and the desktop depends on it.
step "the console will say \"vmuser\""
  until vmuser
  from console
  timeout 180

# THE COMPOSITOR'S OWN BAR. A sway that could not create a backend exits and restarts
# forever, and the bar is the first thing that proves it did not.
step "the console will say \"ARCH-SESSION-STARTING: sway\""
  until "ARCH-SESSION-STARTING: sway"
  from console
  timeout 180

# TWENTY SECONDS AT A DESKTOP, so the recording is a machine that stayed up.
step "wait 20s"
  settle 20

# AND THE PIXELS, FROM THE GUEST'S OWN SCANOUT. `see:` is qemu's screendump of the framebuffer
# virtio-gpu put on the PCIe bus, so a compositor that started but drew nothing is a black,
# uniform frame and fails the check below. MEASURED on this image: a full desktop at 1024x768.
step "read the whole screen as \"screen\""
  read
    name screen

# TWO QUESTIONS ABOUT ONE FRAME, BECAUSE "NOT BLACK" HAS TWO FAILURE SHAPES. A machine with no
# GPU records a uniformly BLACK frame; a compositor that never drew records a uniformly COLOURED
# one, which is not black but is equally empty. MEASURED: a dead frame is mean 0.0 with a mean
# absolute deviation of 0.03, while real desktops here measure mean 2.6..86 and deviation
# 5.0..96 - sway's is the darkest (a black wallpaper with a blue bar, mean 2.6, deviation 5.0).
# So the floors are 1 on both, which is above a dead frame and below every working one.
step "the screen is not black"
  check
    must true
    source screen
    mode mean_between
    name screen_is_not_black
    range [1, 255]
    rect [0, 0, 1024, 768]
    rect_at [1024, 768]

step "the screen is not one flat colour"
  check
    must true
    source screen
    mode spread_between
    name screen_has_content
    range [1, 128]
    rect [0, 0, 1024, 768]
    rect_at [1024, 768]

step "stop the machine by killing it, waiting up to 60s"
  stop kill
  timeout 60
